  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAnother expansive stratus shield currently resides over the\nregion this morning, very similar to Wednesday, with an upper\nshortwave trough now exiting to our east while ridging\nstrengthens over the southern Plains. At the surface,\nnortheasterlies will continue to advect cooler temperatures into\nthe region. Advection in combination with cloud cover will\ntherefore favor a cooler forecast today, especially in areas that\ndon't see clouds mix out and erode as much, but satellite\nobservation shows that much of the region is already beginning to\nsee the stratus scatter out. In all, temperatures will likely\nclimb into the low 80s across the region with portions of\nnorthern Mississippi potentially climbing into the upper 90s.\n\nThe ridge building over the southern Plains will then shift\nslightly east into this weekend, then weaken as an embedded\nshortwave rotates south into the Ohio River Valley around the\nwest side of the upper low to our east. Consequently,\ntemperatures will struggle to rise too far from their present\nlevels into the weekend with afternoon highs generally hanging\nout in the middle 80s. In past forecasts, Sunday has been our\nnext best chance at rainfall, but forecast trends have moved\ntowards a dry forecast with this morning's NBM run displaying\nPoPs below 10% area wide. This is in conjunction with a trend\ntowards ridging to remain somewhat amplified across the southern\nPlains and western Gulf Coast. Into next week, southerly flow\nwill return and bring a quick warming trend Monday and Tuesday.\nThe hottest day of the forecast is increasingly looking to be\nTuesday where NBM highs have a 50% - 70% chance to eclipse\n90 degrees.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, NBM guidance still shows a 40% - 50% chance of\n90+ degree highs. However, the interquartile range for high\ntemperatures jumps drastically to between 15 - 20 degrees during\nthis period. The primary explanation for this increase in\nforecast uncertainty has to do with the interaction between\nHurricane Polo in the eastern Pacific and a trough over the\nRockies. Depending on this interaction, a surface cyclone may\ndevelop over the Plains, which has large implications on the\nweather after Tuesday next week. As of this morning, ensembles\nare split down the middle on either the two systems merging or\nPolo remaining out at sea through the end of the period. If the\ntwo systems are able to phase and produce a surface low pressure\nsystem, then the end of the forecast would trend towards more\nprecipitation late in the forecast. Regardless, the specific\ndetails regarding exact temperatures and precipitation amounts\nafter Tuesday are still extremely uncertain, but forecast\nvariability should rise through this weekend, offering better\ninsight as we approach next week.",
